%I A402195 #10 Nov 15 2026 10:33:01
%S A402195 48182593,48182867,48182929,48182951,48183013,48183019,48183061,
%T A402195 48183103,48183119,48183133,48183631,48183721,48183823,48184277,
%U A402195 48184291,48184321,48184369,48184519,48184973,48184991,48185113,48185131,48185233,48185257,48185279,48185287,48185353
%N A402195 Primes p for which pi_{24;5,7}(p) - pi_{24;1,11}(p) = -1, where pi_{m;a,b,...}(x) is the number of primes <= x which are congruent to a or b or ... (mod m).
%C A402195 This sequence is about one of the 21 prime races modulo 24 between cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see A401958 for more details. Specifically, here we have H = {1,11} and S = {5,7}, corresponding to T = {chi_{-8}, chi_12}, where chi_d = (d/.) is the Kronecker symbol viewed as a character modulo N = 24.
%C A402195 This sequence gives primes p such that Sum_{primes q<=p} (chi_{-8}(q) + chi_12(q))/2 = 1.
%C A402195 Assuming GRH and LI (see A401958), the set of x with pi_{24;5,7}(x) > pi_{24;1,11}(x) has logarithmic density 0.99477, making the race between {1,11} and {5,7} the 11th most biased among the 21 races of cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see the Zenodo preprint "Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of a subgroup of index 4 modulo 24" below.
%C A402195 Let D(x) = pi_{24;5,7}(x) - pi_{24;1,11}(x). Below x0 = 8*10^12 there are respectively 34876145777, 34876274835, 34876248066, 34876268167, 34876282644, 34876258940, 34876298151, and 34876294229 primes congruent to 1, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, or 23 modulo 24, giving D(x0) = 108957. There are 4 sign-changing zones giving a total of 77933 terms below x0:
%C A402195   (1) a(1..400) = 48182593-48258139 (there are 2215 primes a(1) <= p <= a(400) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -24 at p = 48211067);
%C A402195   (2) a(401..441) = 5237746259-5237763101 (there are 92 primes a(401) <= p <= a(441) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -4 at p = 5237747051);
%C A402195   (3) a(442..17516) = 184309575083-193985021701 (there are 295346931 primes a(442) <= p <= a(17516) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -8635 at p = 189028438513);
%C A402195   (4) a(17517..77933) = 922317124739-924430682129 (there are 36284767 primes a(17517) <= p <= a(77933) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -3052 at p = 922573975283).
%C A402195 The total number of primes p <= x0 with D(p) < 0 is 331634005.
%H A402195 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A402195/b402195.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..77933</a> (all terms below 8*10^12)
%H A402195 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24020025">Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of a subgroup of index 4 modulo 24</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A402195 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A402193/a402193_1.txt">Sign-changing zones of five prime number races modulo 24</a>.
%H A402195 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24159887">Sign-changing zones of prime number races between cosets modulo 24, and of pi(x;8,5) - pi(x;8,1), up to 7*10^12</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A402195 Greg Martin and Daniel Fiorilli, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Chennai-31Aug10.pdf">Prime number races: An asymptotic formula for the densities</a>, Analytic and Combinatorial Number Theory, Institute of Mathematical Sciences, Chennai, India. August 31, 2010.
%H A402195 Greg Martin, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Canberra-29Nov18.pdf">Prime number races</a>, Pure Math Seminar, UNSW Canberra. November 29, 2018.
%H A402195 Andrey S. Shchebetov, <a href="https://math101.guru/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/01-A3-Presentation-v7.3EN-no.pdf">Testing Chebyshev's bias for prime numbers up to 10^15</a>, EUCYS 2018.
%H A402195 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chebyshev%27s_bias">Chebyshev's bias</a>.
%o A402195 (PARI) my(k=0); forprime(p=2, 1e11, if(p%24==1||p%24==11, k--); if(p%24==5||p%24==7, k++); if(k==-1, print1(p, ", ")))
%Y A402195 See A401958 for related sequences.
%K A402195 nonn,new
%O A402195 1,1
%A A402195 _Leandro Hector Gallego_ and _Jianing Song_, Nov 15 2026
